// parity-expected-stdout-begin
// [1, 1, 2, 3]
// ["a", "a", "b"]
// parity-expected-stdout-end

// KI-098 — owner: 0.0.8 plan step 2a. Top-level functions fixed; module
// members are step 2a-4.
//
// NOTE: this program compiles, runs, and prints the right answer. There is no
// error to show. The loss it pins is in the generated code, not the
// behaviour, so no test, parity sweep or ordinary snapshot here can see it.
//
// The only difference from ki_098_baseline_one_instantiation.flx is the second
// call, at `List<String>`. B1 restores the representation only where every
// call site agrees on one concrete instantiation, so used at two types,
// `copy_head` alone would stay generalized and its element binder would lose
// `IntRep`:
//
//     baseline (one call):   ::(h#1302:Int, t#1303:Box) →
//     two calls, generic:    ::(h#1302,     t#1303:Box) →
//
// Step 2a clones it per instantiation instead. The `List<Int>` call goes to
// `copy_head$Int`, which has the baseline's representation; the
// `List<String>` call keeps the generic body, since every boxed type has the
// same representation.
//
// Observe it with:
//
//     FLUX_DBG_SPEC=1 cargo run -- --dump-core=debug examples/generics/failing/degraded/ki_098_despecialised_two_instantiations.flx
//
// Pinned by `snapshot_ki_098_despecialised_core_def` in
// tests/aether/cli_snapshots.rs, which extracts `copy_head$Int`. It matches
// the baseline's snapshot apart from the definition's name.

fn copy_head(xs) {
    match xs {
        [h | t] -> [h | [h | t]],
        _ -> [],
    }
}

fn main() with IO {
    print(copy_head([1, 2, 3]))
    print(copy_head(["a", "b"]))
}
